::GEO_U06_L05_GP01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L05_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.14<br><br>Question:<br>A rectangular prism is 6 cm by 4 cm by 5 cm. What is its volume? {
=A. 120 cm&sup3;#Volume is lwh, so 6(4)(5)=120 cubic centimeters.
~B. 30 cm&sup2;#This adds dimensions and uses area units.
~C. 48 cm&sup3;#This multiplies only two dimensions by 2, not all three dimensions.
~D. 15 cm#This adds dimensions and uses linear units.
}

::GEO_U06_L05_GP02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L05_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.14<br><br>Question:<br>A cylinder has radius 3 and height 7. Which expression gives its volume? {
~A. 2&pi;(3)(7)#This resembles lateral surface area, not volume.
=B. &pi;(3)&sup2;(7)#Cylinder volume is &pi;r&sup2;h.
~C. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>&pi;(3)&sup2;(7)#The one-third factor is for a cone, not a cylinder.
~D. 4&pi;(3)&sup3;#This is related to sphere volume, not cylinder volume.
}

::GEO_U06_L05_GP03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L05_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.14<br><br>Question:<br>A cone and cylinder have the same radius and height. How does the cone volume compare to the cylinder volume? {
~A. It is equal to the cylinder volume#A cone is not equal in volume to the matching cylinder.
~B. It is twice the cylinder volume#A cone is smaller than the matching cylinder.
=C. It is one-third of the cylinder volume#Cone volume is <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>&pi;r&sup2;h.
~D. It is three times the cylinder volume#This reverses the relationship.
}

::GEO_U06_L05_GP04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L05_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.14<br><br>Question:<br>Which unit is appropriate for volume? {
~A. cm#Centimeters are linear units.
~B. cm&sup2;#Square centimeters measure area or surface area.
~C. degrees#Degrees measure angles.
=D. cm&sup3;#Volume measures three-dimensional space, so cubic units are appropriate.
}

::GEO_U06_L05_GP05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L05_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.14<br><br>Question:<br>A sphere has radius 2. Which expression gives its volume? {
=A. <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>&pi;(2)&sup3;#Sphere volume is <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>&pi;r&sup3;.
~B. 4&pi;(2)&sup2;#This is sphere surface area, not volume.
~C. &pi;(2)&sup2;h#This is cylinder volume and includes height.
~D. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>&pi;(2)&sup2;h#This is cone volume and includes height.
}
